PROJECT-SPECIFIC TERMS AND ATTRIBUTION

1. Project scope

This license applies to the original source code, notebook code, scripts,
configuration files, documentation, figures created by the project, and other
original materials in this repository, unless a file states otherwise.

2. Third-party data and materials

The repository may contain, reference, or process datasets and materials
provided by third parties, including NASA GISTEMP, the India Meteorological
Department (IMD), NOAA Climate Prediction Center ONI data, NASA POWER, and
other external sources. Those materials are not relicensed by this MIT License.
Users must review and comply with the original provider's terms, licenses,
usage restrictions, citation requirements, and redistribution policies before
copying, publishing, or redistributing third-party data.

3. Third-party software

The project uses open-source Python packages, including NumPy, pandas, SciPy,
Matplotlib, Seaborn, scikit-learn, statsmodels, XGBoost, SHAP, pymannkendall,
xarray, netCDF4, openpyxl, Jupyter, and related dependencies. Each dependency
remains subject to its own license. Nothing in this file grants rights beyond
those provided by the applicable dependency license.

4. Research and attribution

If this repository, its code, figures, tables, or derived results are used in
academic work, publications, presentations, teaching materials, or public
reports, users are requested to acknowledge the project and cite the relevant
underlying data providers. A suggested acknowledgement is:

"This work uses the India Climate Data Science project by Maharshi K Patel,
including its reproducible climate-data analysis, statistical methods,
machine-learning workflow, and forecasting outputs. Underlying data sources
include NASA GISTEMP, the India Meteorological Department, NOAA Climate
Prediction Center ONI data, NASA POWER, and other sources identified in the
repository documentation."

5. Results and interpretation

The analyses, forecasts, figures, and model outputs are provided for research,
educational, and exploratory purposes. They are not climate-policy advice,
operational forecasts, emergency guidance, financial advice, or a substitute
for official meteorological information. Users are responsible for validating
inputs, methods, assumptions, and results before relying on them.

6. No warranty for data-derived outputs

The MIT warranty disclaimer applies to the original software. Because the
project processes external data and uses statistical and machine-learning
models, the author makes no guarantee that any generated table, figure,
forecast, classification, or interpretation is complete, current, error-free,
or suitable for a particular purpose.

7. Changes and derivatives

Modified versions and derivative works may be distributed under different
terms, provided that the original copyright notice and applicable license
notice are preserved and changes are clearly identified. Users should retain
attribution to the original project when distributing substantial derivatives.

